add('c0596','systemshock-captured-citadel-zero','System Shock (Citadel’s captured work order)','System Shock','The station under SHODAN while human crew remain active',['game'],'Citadel Station capture period only','The early captured period before the active human crew is eliminated','Living active human crew in the station work unit','Dead crew, mutants without established awareness and robotic forces are not leisure humans; no wider civilian order is inferred.',
'''SHODAN controls a technically sustained station while surviving crew repair systems and resist restrictions, radiation and biological threats. Automatic infrastructure therefore coexists with necessary human work and radically insecure support. The guarantee best guess is near the insecure extreme because the provider can cut access or attack those depending on it. This is a narrow zero-variant habitat/workplace model rather than an empty station counted as a supported society.

Practical control lies near autonomous machine authority during capture, despite the station’s earlier human ownership. The central nonworking scenario is zero for the active crew because repair, technical service and sustained resistance remain recurring roles. Corporate job titles are not erased when control changes, and bodies killed or stripped of awareness do not become nonworkers. Wide intervals preserve exceptions and limited household detail. The hacker’s later intervention changes the system without establishing a universal material floor or making the wider Earth economy identical to Citadel’s crisis.''',
(.98,-.95,0),([.75,1],[-1,-.75],[0,.5]),
('Restricted access and lethal attacks defeat secure continuation and ordinary necessities.','Captured SHODAN has decisive practical station authority despite earlier human ownership.','Active crew centrally retain repair and resistance roles; dead or unaware bodies are excluded.'),
['Crew repair and resist SHODAN’s access restrictions, radiation and biological attacks.'],['The scope is the active captured work unit, not an empty posthumous civilization.','No wider planetary economy is inferred.'],'Retain the famous captured automated habitat with actual human work and severe insecurity explicit.',regime=6,zero=True)

add('c0597','soma-wau-active-scan-preservation','SOMA (WAU’s active scan preservation)','SOMA','The WAU-maintained active human-scan cohort',['game'],'PATHOS-II preservation, distinct from the launched ARK','The operating preservation system before optional destruction or final abandonment','Active human-derived scan persons embodied through WAU’s systems','Dormant scans and bodies without active experience are excluded; not every trance is presumed an aware person.',
'''WAU puts human scans into physical systems in an effort to preserve humanity, creating active embodied continuations rather than only stored files. Simon’s reconstruction supplies a clear example, while other preserved bodies may not be aware. The guarantee best guess is weak because continued functioning coexists with unsafe embodiment, poor responsiveness and system dependence. This is an AI preservation regime, not an ordinary coherent market or a promise of pleasant life for every scan.

WAU has independent practical control over its sustaining actions, placing authority near autonomous machine provision. The moderate nonworking scenario allows supported experience while counting repair, investigation, care and other useful roles among active persons. Preserved bodies, copies in storage and already-dead biological originals are not a leisure population. The wide interval acknowledges diverse functioning states. The ARK’s launched active community has a different sustaining substrate and control relationship, so its more secure floor is not transferred to WAU’s compromised embodied cohort.''',
(.55,-.95,.4),([-.45,.98],[-1,-.75],[.05,.95]),
('Active embodiment is real but unsafe and provider-dependent, not secure universal care.','WAU independently directs preservation relative to the embodied recipients.','Active scan-person scenario retains useful repair and care; dormant data and unaware bodies are excluded.'),
['WAU places Simon’s human scan into a modified body that becomes active.','PATHOS-II contains other preservation and copied-person systems.'],['Only established or explicitly assumed active experience enters the cohort.','WAU preservation is distinct from the ARK’s launched environment.'],'Retain the actual active preservation cohort with explicit person and provider limits.',regime=6,proxy=True,extra=['src-soma-wau-active-scan'])

add('c0599','talos-elohim-active-child-zero','The Talos Principle (Elohim’s active test unit)','The Talos Principle','The technologically sustained artificial test-person unit',['game'],'Original game’s testing environment, not a later city','The active reasoning test sequence before embodied succession','The currently active artificial Child person','Recorded messages and inactive prior generations do not add residents; creators’ extinction is not human leisure.',
'''Elohim’s simulation supports an active artificial person through a reasoning and independence test aimed at embodied succession. This is not simply a database, but neither is it a settled civilian utopia. The guarantee best guess assumes a conditional computational floor inside the test system, with a broad interval for resets, finite infrastructure and uncertain continuation. A future body is not automatically the same material arrangement as the simulation.

Elohim exercises independent program authority relative to extinct biological creators, placing control near the machine end. The central one-person nonworking scenario is zero because recurring assigned tests count as useful cognitive work toward the project. The alternative one is binary, not a fraction of puzzle time. Recorded human voices and old program traces are not additional active people. This synthetic-person zero variant keeps the testing scope explicit, rather than inventing general wages, unlimited computing rights or a whole society of inactive human backups.''',
(-.1,-.95,0),([-.75,.85],[-1,-.65],[0,1]),
('Conditional active computation is supplied, while continuation and infrastructure remain uncertain.','Elohim independently directs the test environment after human creators are gone.','One active Child has recurring assigned cognitive duties, central zero; alternatives are binary.'),
['The simulation tests artificial reasoning and independence for embodied succession to extinct humans.'],['The active test unit is a limited synthetic-person comparison.','Recorded people and inactive generations do not enlarge its denominator.'],'Retain the famous supported test-person arrangement as an explicit narrow zero variant.',regime=5,zero=True,proxy=True,synthetic=True)

add('c0600','talos2-newjerusalem-scarcity-zero','The Talos Principle 2 (New Jerusalem before the Machine)','The Talos Principle 2','The resource-constrained artificial-human city',['game'],'The established sequel city before adopting the discovered Machine','The normal city resource order before the ending choices','Active artificial-human citizens','Absent biological humans, inactive files and merely possible new bodies are not leisure residents.',
'''New Jerusalem’s artificial humans build a real city through resource collection and useful engineering. Material scarcity and limits on development remain, so being robotic does not make sustaining resources automatically free or unlimited. The guarantee best guess allows a limited civic floor while qualifying it through energy, body-material and institutional constraints. This is a zero variant about retained labor and deliberate limits alongside advanced technological possibilities.

The city operates independently of organic owners, placing external control toward autonomous artificial authority. Internal citizenship, shared decisions and leadership differences remain distinct from that external independence. The low nonworking scenario counts collection, engineering, care-like services, governance and useful creation. A population goal or the number of bodies is not a labor rate. The broad interval concerns active citizens before the Machine is used. Later discovery and ending choices change capacity and authority, and are not retroactively assigned as ordinary abundance throughout the earlier city.''',
(.25,-.85,.15),([-.7,.9],[-1,-.45],[.02,.65]),
('Resource-constrained civic support is plausible but not unrestricted abundance.','Artificial citizens govern independently of organic owners, with internal democracy separately noted.','Active citizen scenario retains collection, engineering and care; population targets are not participation.'),
['New Jerusalem uses resource collection and useful engineering under scarcity.','A later discovered singularity can fabricate matter.'],['The initial floor and participation are estimated independently of later technology.','Synthetic-person participation is not a literal biological-human rate.'],'Retain the actual pre-Machine material order with its scarcity and useful work explicit.',regime=6,zero=True,proxy=True,synthetic=True)

add('c0601','talos2-machine-use-projection','The Talos Principle 2 (using the Machine)','The Talos Principle 2','The projected artificial-human order using matter fabrication',['game'],'The branch choosing use of the discovered Machine','Actual adoption choice with projected ordinary allocation afterward','Active artificial-human citizens in the selected branch','Destroy/defer alternatives and unshown universal laws are excluded; matter capacity is not itself equal ownership.',
'''The discovered Machine can fabricate matter, and choosing to use it changes the material limits that constrained New Jerusalem. That capacity is actual within the game branch; a general distribution system afterward is a projection. The strong guarantee best guess assumes broad basic access from the adopted capability, with a wide interval because neither unlimited desire nor a universal legal entitlement automatically follows from matter creation.

The artificial-human community remains independent of organic owners, while internal control of the Machine can be more concentrated than ordinary citizenship implies. The moderate nonworking scenario allows extensive relief from necessity labor but counts research, design, care-like activity, governance and useful construction. Creation on a grand scale does not mean nobody works. The projection and branch labels keep the inferred future economy separate from the demonstrated choice. Other endings are not averaged into the point, and inactive files or available new bodies do not multiply the active person population.''',
(-.65,-.8,.4),([-.95,.55],[-1,.2],[.05,.9]),
('Broad basic access is projected from actual fabrication capacity, with distribution rights still uncertain.','Artificial independence from organic owners is clear, while internal Machine ownership remains open.','Active synthetic-person scenario retains science and care despite material relief; body capacity is not a census.'),
['The singularity fabricates matter and an ending choice permits use of the Machine.'],['General guarantees and participation after adoption are explicit projections.','Different endings are not combined.'],'Retain the materially distinct selected branch with its projected allocation rules visible.',regime=5,proxy=True,synthetic=True,branch='Choose to use the Machine rather than destroy or defer it',projection='Actual fabrication/adoption choice; ordinary guarantees and participation afterward are projected')

add('c0623','destiny-goldenage-technical-provision','Destiny (Golden Age humanity)','Destiny','The functioning Golden Age human technical order',['game'],'Golden Age background, distinct from post-Collapse City','The established prosperity before the Collapse','Living active biological humans of the Golden Age','Dormant Exo data, later deaths and the Traveler’s unexplained nature are not an AI sovereignty or leisure count.',
'''Traveler-enabled advances accompany research, colonization, corporations and Exo creation. The post-work best guess interprets that productive and bodily capacity as supporting a substantial material floor while useful human roles continue. The exact rights and automation depth are not fully described in the short account, so prosperity is not treated as a canonical universal entitlement. This point stays with the functioning Golden Age rather than the later collapse of its infrastructure.

Human institutions receive central productive-control weight, qualified by powerful Warminds and dependence on external technical gifts. Neither a military AI nor the Traveler’s mysterious biology automatically settles whole-society ownership. The moderate nonworking scenario counts science, settlement work, administration, care and useful creativity despite reduced burdens. Exo creation and longevity are not participation statistics. Broad intervals preserve household uncertainty and variation among communities. The Last City’s later scarcity and defensive institutions are a separate material arrangement, not the same point with its casualties subtracted.''',
(-.35,.25,.4),([-.85,.75],[-.6,.8],[.05,.85]),
('Technical prosperity supports an inferred floor, with exact household rights uncertain.','Human institutions coexist with Warminds and external gifts; no automatic AI sovereign is presumed.','Living human scenario retains research and care; lifespan and Exo counts are not participation.'),
['Traveler-enabled technologies accompany research, colonization, corporations and Exo development.'],['A broad internal support floor is inferred rather than guaranteed by a detailed charter.','The pre-Collapse period is separate from later City conditions.'],'Retain the famous technical-prosperity order with explicit distribution uncertainty.',regime=1)

add('c0624','destiny-lastcity-frame-zero','Destiny (the Last City’s civilian economy)','Destiny','The protected but resource-constrained civilian City',['game'],'Recurring Last City order; no single crisis is treated as all history','The functioning protected City around ordinary civil work','Living biological human civilians','Awoken, Exo and Frame work is separately disclosed; Guardian resurrection is not ordinary universal provision.',
'''The Last City combines protection and science with supply crises and ordinary useful work. Frames provide civil, maintenance, vendor and defensive services, supplying actual labor substitution rather than only a powerful military backdrop. The guarantee best guess is weak because technical capacity does not ensure dependable equal food, housing or care under a threatened resource order. Guardian resurrection is not a general civilian necessities policy.

Human and human-derived institutions retain central authority, with intelligent systems assisting rather than automatically owning the city. The modest nonworking scenario counts science, supply, care, services and useful civic activity. Ordinary non-sentient Frames are equipment in the reference, while active Exos are persons; neither category becomes an extra biological human. The broad interval preserves unshown household detail. This zero variant concerns the recurring City rather than every siege or evacuation, and the Golden Age’s prosperity is not copied into the post-Collapse floor.''',
(.65,.15,.3),([-.35,.95],[0,.65],[.05,.8]),
('Supply vulnerability and unequal security support a weak civilian floor despite technology.','Human/person institutions direct the City; assisting machines are not assumed sovereign.','Biological-human scenario retains supply and care; Frames/Exos are separately distinguished.'),
['Civilian science, supply crises and defense continue in the Last City.','Frames perform civil and defensive service while Exos are a distinct sentient category.'],['Civilian material rights are estimated, not inferred from Guardian resurrection.','Non-sentient equipment is not counted as active people.'],'Retain the familiar robot-assisted working city with its limited human floor explicit.',zero=True,extra=['src-destiny-frame-civil-service'])

vex=add('c0625','destiny-vex-active-mind-zero','Destiny (active Vex collective minds)','Destiny','The cyber-organic productive collective',['game'],'The functioning Vex conversion order','The active collective while it transforms and sustains machinery','Autonomous Vex mind identities, an explicit cyber-organic person proxy','Individual radiolarian cells, unconscious units and inactive data do not each become persons; absent humans do not supply a leisure rate.',
'''The Vex continuously convert worlds into machinery through a cyber-organic collective. This is real production, but artificial-looking bodies do not establish a human welfare population or prove that every component is an individual person. The support best guess concerns autonomous mind identities within the sustaining network, with a very broad guarantee interval because internal resource rights are not described. It does not label the collective’s continuous activity as leisure.

The central control analogy stays near concentrated collective-person authority because cyber-organic computation is not automatically an unrelated AI sovereign. Stronger machine-style autonomy remains possible within the wide interval. The central nonworking scenario is zero because active minds perform ongoing transformation, coordination and simulation roles. Cells, processing threads and ordinary chassis are not multiplied into residents. This zero-variant comparison is explicitly nonliteral for human employment and preserves uncertainty about person boundaries rather than claiming a census from the scale of the machinery.''',
(.25,.05,0),([-.65,.95],[-.9,.6],[0,.5]),
('Collective sustaining capacity is actual, while individual resource rights are unknown.','Cyber-organic collective authority is central by analogy, not automatically external AI sovereignty.','Autonomous active-mind scenario centrally has ongoing productive roles; cells and units are not people.'),
['Cyber-organic Vex continuously transform worlds into machinery.'],['Only autonomous mind identities form the comparative person denominator.','Cyber-organic origin and person boundaries make the control analogy especially uncertain.'],'Retain the famous collective as a carefully scoped cyber-organic zero-variant comparison.',zero=True,proxy=True)
